## Week 12 – Question 9: Student Marks Indexing System

Problem Statement:

Create a Student Marks Indexing System using a HashMap. Store each student's ID, name, and marks. Allow the user to search for a student using their ID and display the student's details if found.

### Java Program

```

import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

        int n = sc.nextInt();

        HashMap<Integer, String> names = new HashMap<>();
        HashMap<Integer, Integer> marks = new HashMap<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int id = sc.nextInt();
            String name = sc.next();
            int mark = sc.nextInt();

            names.put(id, name);
            marks.put(id, mark);
        }

        int searchId = sc.nextInt();

        if (names.containsKey(searchId)) {
            System.out.println("Student ID: " + searchId);
            System.out.println("Name: " + names.get(searchId));
            System.out.println("Marks: " + marks.get(searchId));
        } else {
            System.out.println("Student not found");
        }

        sc.close();
    }
}

```

### Input

```
3
101 Ravi 85
102 Priya 92
103 Arun 78
102
```

### Output

```
Student ID: 102
Name: Priya
Marks: 92
```

### Explanation

- The program stores student IDs, names, and marks using `HashMap`.
- The user searches for student ID `102`.
- The program finds Priya's details and displays her marks.

Note: HashMap allows efficient lookup of student details using the student ID.
